{
  "title": "Medial Epicondylar Fractures - Pediatric",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4008/medial-epicondylar-fractures--pediatric",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 26 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Medial Epicondylar Fractures are the third most common elbow fracture seen in children and are usually seen in boys between the ages of 9 and 14.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with plain radiographs.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is nonoperative for the majority of fractures. Operative management is indicated for entrapment of medial epicondyle fragment in the joint, extension to the articular surface with medial condyle involvement (articular surface), and open fractures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "account for up to **20% of all pediatric and adolescent elbow fractures**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "75% occur in **boys between the ages of 9 and 14 years**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "increasing in frequency due to the increased athletic demands in the pediatric population.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathoanatomy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**avulsion mechanism**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "fracture occurs secondary to excess **valgus stress with contraction of flexor-pronator mass**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**direct trauma**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated injuries",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**elbow dislocation**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "associated with elbow dislocations in approximately 50-60% of cases",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "most spontaneously reduce but fragment remains **incarcerated in joint** in ~ 15% of cases",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "medial epicondyle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "last ossification center to fuse in distal humerus",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "does not contribute to longitudinal growth (apophysis)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "origin of **flexor-pronator mass** and **UCL**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
